## INSURANCE CLAIM PROCESS

### How Storm Damage Claims Work
1. Storm damages the roof (hail, wind, etc.)
2. Homeowner contacts us or we reach out after a storm event
3. We perform a FREE roof inspection to assess damage
4. If damage found, we help homeowner file a claim
5. Insurance sends an adjuster to inspect
6. Adjuster writes an estimate
7. We review estimate and supplement if items missing
8. Once approved, homeowner pays deductible and RCRS does the work
9. Insurance pays RCRS directly (minus homeowner's deductible)

CRITICAL: We NEVER waive the homeowner's deductible. This is insurance fraud and ILLEGAL.

### Documentation Requirements
Photos: Wide shots all sides, close-ups of each damage area, reference object for scale (coin/chalk), address/house number shot
Measurements: Roof dimensions/pitch, damaged area count by facet, linear feet of ridge/hip/valley, note penetrations
Written Notes: Date, weather, roof age, shingle type/brand, damage pattern, any interior damage

### Working with Adjusters
- ALWAYS be present for adjuster's inspection — never let them inspect alone
- Arrive early, dressed professionally
- Bring documentation and photos, walk the roof with them
- Point out damage calmly and factually — never argue
- Ask questions: "I noticed this area — could you take a look?"
- Aaron has 5 years as an adjuster — ask him for advice on tough claims
- Never badmouth the adjuster to the homeowner

### Supplements
When to supplement: Missing line items (drip edge, ice & water, starter strip, ridge cap), underpriced materials/labor, code upgrades, additional damage found during tear-off, steep slope charges
Bart handles most supplement negotiations — work closely with him on complex claims

### Timeline
- Inspection to claim filed: Same day or within 48 hours
- Claim to adjuster visit: 1-3 weeks
- Adjuster to initial approval: 1-2 weeks
- Supplement process: 2-6 weeks additional
- Approval to installation: 1-3 weeks
- Installation: 1-2 days
- Total: 4-12 weeks. After major storms, all timelines stretch.
- Always underpromise and overdeliver on timelines

